 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d008: cascade (k=8)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 10.9 and radius 1.76 with 8 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.49) and a 4-dim charge; 10 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 17 couplings (35% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.93 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.12 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.5 particles, 13.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.071 vs ESN 1.000\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.077  random bonds 0.070\n  xor      0.627 vs ESN 0.665\u00b10.237  [match]  no bonds 0.627  random bonds 0.627\n  narma10  0.236 vs ESN 0.842\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.262  random bonds 0.232\n  latch    0.966 vs ESN 0.677\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.963  random bonds 0.966\n  mnist14  0.576 vs ESN 0.839\u00b10.006  [below]  no bonds 0.557  random bonds 0.570\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
